00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.950
Migrating your on-premises
databases to the cloud

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.980
is a fantastic way to reduce
costs, improve reliability,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.660
and expand your scale.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.060
But the process of moving
that data isn't always

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:30.180
straightforward, which is why
our teams here at Google Cloud

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.460
have some best
practices to share.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.950
Dominic Preuss walks you
through the three main stages

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.070
of data and infrastructure
migration-- assessment,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.860
migration, and
validation-- each of which

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.140
have their own specific nuances.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.700
Let's start with
assessment where

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.890
it's important to first
discover all the information

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.620
about your data, including
what you're planning to move

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.110
and what dependencies
are involved with that.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.660
Then you need to learn about
the capabilities of the cloud

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.000
platform services you
are moving to in order

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.150
to understand feature priority
and performance, at which time

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.490
you can finally data mine
the right path forward

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.170
for your migration.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.250
Now once you have
completed your assessment,

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.220
it's critical to evaluate
for each of your databases

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.220
what migration type you do.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.960
This includes looking
at each database

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.670
and asking questions
like if you still

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.820
need it, if it should be
replaced by another system,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.990
rehosted, refactored,
revised, or even rebuilt.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.540
This helps you plan on
exactly the type of work that

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.390
needs to be done for
each of your databases.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.380
Now from a more
practical perspective,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.470
it's important to ask
yourself whether or not

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.000
your database can
handle downtime

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.990
during a migration process.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.620
For example, if you can
stop writes to your existing

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.070
database, export your data to
a resource like cloud storage,

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.570
and then import into
your new database,

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:45.900
then this is going to be a
less complicated and rather

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.700
straightforward process.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.860
To speed up offline
migration, the talk

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.590
touches on a couple of
performance hacks you can do.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.665
In short, optimize your schema,
choose powerful instances

00:01:55.665 --> 00:01:58.230
for your import,
and monitor progress

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.860
to adjust along the way.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.770
If you can't allow for
downtime, then you'll

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.600
need to rely on replication
to get the job done.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.450
This is where you replicate
your data to the new database.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.479
And once the data is
fully replicated--

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.120
meaning the lag between the
old and the new database is

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:12.900
minimal--

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.080
you can simply do a scheduled
failover towards a new database

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.870
with no or minimal
downtime incurred.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.310
If you do a
heterogeneous migration,

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.070
there are a number of
partner applications

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.100
that can help you with
the live migrations.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.780
Once you have
migrated, it's finally

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.420
time for the validation
step, which first includes

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.330
ensuring that all the data
has come across properly

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.490
and then validating that
your performance and cost

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.490
expectations are still accurate
with your new cloud-enabled

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.170
databases.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.570
If you're interested in more
information about migrating

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:45.600
from Cassandra to Cloud
Bigtable or Cloud Spanner,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.630
as well as a great customer
profile with many live demos

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.330
on how they accomplished
database migrations,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.250
then check out the full talk.

